#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129018 :question/id #uuid "f31dd085-70fa-5eed-adf9-89fb0e6654e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7348" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/05/051cfbbf3fd37f01ff7e4442fccdc5ec4ce506e3c452b68bfa2ed5ae6e5cf6b9.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({2}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32({sin}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{sin}^{3}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707178 :question-generator/id #uuid "413b8d2f-4ff1-5ca7-be76-5c254b747050" :question-generator/script "generators/61/619ce761e20ddca7c906d2878f304b283955ae1c8e7ac7e24ab8fa9507d71e86/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbb1193-47be-5246-a935-a3e2f7a5d9cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321372} {:answer/id #uuid "6be44563-7ff5-5aff-888b-8534ab2d8574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321988} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81d17b-e0e4-5462-aa1e-2daeda378c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322134} {:answer/id #uuid "9e663173-e8bf-5e07-9a64-eabd853d519d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322395} {:answer/id #uuid "c307b8a7-c480-5cfb-a7fb-ea588c87cd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c307b8a7-c480-5cfb-a7fb-ea588c87cd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322662} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b4bbfb-a31e-55b3-9e9c-d9e99a687a4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323330}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a604ede7-8a36-5960-82df-176ca647b425" :question/math-academy-id "q-38140" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd51e610-6f03-5601-be97-90a082867f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128804} {:answer/id #uuid "115c217e-8e0a-5c5b-8302-ae521adc07f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128805} {:answer/id #uuid "232b05d7-2819-5154-96f7-710edeff9ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128806} {:answer/id #uuid "4505c771-cef0-508f-89e7-d4849ae94ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128807} {:answer/id #uuid "6325e896-5085-50b5-ab25-23a0ebf6535e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd51e610-6f03-5601-be97-90a082867f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128804} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8292c76-a1e0-5537-aaa6-9b5b73963ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "929376bc-8d45-5684-ae94-b99b8cb09d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-83968" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡0-{sin}^{5}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e776117-b2c8-5c7a-9d16-751f765be404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275599} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9d1ae7-f6c6-570b-9ced-645ab8a2169a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276190} {:answer/id #uuid "7c016cad-a688-555e-9bb3-a399d13f0149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276608} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2b4cc3-f053-5ed5-b9d6-7f1939263729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276859} {:answer/id #uuid "faf68c35-0933-5c2f-838a-cc6c8b57001f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf68c35-0933-5c2f-838a-cc6c8b57001f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277676} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c7f3a1e-076c-5684-8e63-15c2040c9f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277749}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fa6b6f5-4249-530e-aeb1-5511d96e1746" :question/math-academy-id "q-161050" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=15{x}^{2}y$ over the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/9b/9b54b7a488a1983ba1816fdf5c55e039951eb417102c61963977b2f48b9c8d9e.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 1,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}15({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )(1-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}(2\\pi )-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}(0)) \\\\  & =-(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad79092-f016-53f7-a552-da7705b0e553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275728} {:answer/id #uuid "7636b055-40d1-5da4-992b-6cf4130c550f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276544} {:answer/id #uuid "bed5cbac-2b75-59e8-bf61-95fb98959df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277178} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd67c0a-3dac-561a-8db6-3731e772cb7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277422} {:answer/id #uuid "f22075cf-3f26-53dc-89c2-6eefbee0d8bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed5cbac-2b75-59e8-bf61-95fb98959df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277178} :answer-field/id #uuid "f825344c-a3e7-5d9c-a7f6-4fa9aa047ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278162}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad9dc383-588b-5128-942d-8b56b5fb2221" :question/math-academy-id "q-161052" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=4{y}^{2}$ over the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}4(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}{[{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta (81-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116fe698-da44-5921-9595-81cea6cf0777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi ({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275632} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb65dd3-22d7-5680-9fd1-2950d61da9b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}(2\\pi -1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275734} {:answer/id #uuid "63ccd069-a545-55f8-89a3-8f411148b91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276380} {:answer/id #uuid "7d759d00-8c72-5816-95c1-40378ed648d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276620} {:answer/id #uuid "b249fdf3-d35b-5850-8f01-7cb45187798e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b249fdf3-d35b-5850-8f01-7cb45187798e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi ({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277073} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd14850f-a094-504b-a27e-fd0a9ea939b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278114}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bba8bb12-bb44-5085-86bc-e29c01145558" :question/math-academy-id "q-161053" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ over the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/81/811cfa127ad232e8d5a990998604718a57344d1bd7f43173dc0987631c13db33.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{(r{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1)(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a04819ad-b64f-5339-bd10-f5dae67b0b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276922} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2c3493-79bf-50d5-b228-17395a6342de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277160} {:answer/id #uuid "e51cd9aa-028b-5139-bde8-c1e863ead1cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277477} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0a5b30-f93d-53d1-a5dd-3f5cd120beb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277664} {:answer/id #uuid "fb94ab63-cb9a-57db-88b5-198ca54a362e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e51cd9aa-028b-5139-bde8-c1e863ead1cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277477} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3194bfd-1758-55d9-8263-07b55c8c710d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278069}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f641b0ca-15bb-53b1-96d0-217b8812f929" :question/math-academy-id "q-161054" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ over the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}2{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{81}{4}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d7209c-7c17-506b-a4a6-86dde4f2d563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275501} {:answer/id #uuid "22dc0a44-0404-58da-88dd-666cf5f8fdf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275788} {:answer/id #uuid "24f5786b-4836-55d8-ad56-141581c6fdc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275810} {:answer/id #uuid "3edc9dc7-a45f-5d0a-8152-3cecbb7db9af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276022} {:answer/id #uuid "60d9ad63-0c6e-5759-b321-954fef286724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24f5786b-4836-55d8-ad56-141581c6fdc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275810} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3c6d819-dfad-57d8-acd9-b4103349034b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278038}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4a72efd-94bd-57c9-9125-f124ca8a7f81" :question/math-academy-id "q-161055" :question/problem "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }(2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -0)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c8b8e0-2cc2-5fe5-a1df-e98cd1001f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351613} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4bd1d1-6340-5981-bf6a-672c766b04a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352654} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e43f8a-2b13-5ebe-b51e-312ffdb9f42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353302} {:answer/id #uuid "de54c0b4-a6ca-5c07-9dbe-5dedc4f5b648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353496} {:answer/id #uuid "fa31fa69-bb59-5f48-98b0-b5486fce2c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa31fa69-bb59-5f48-98b0-b5486fce2c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353757} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbf98d97-f14f-5123-b9e9-537f4bff4e00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354139}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a65ae57-db8c-559b-a30a-84cd82c23ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83970" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ over the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\le 0$ and $y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/e4/e4275e74a45deb22c0ed22546772e33fbd5caeb89a4036b1a2f4870e8ecf67a1.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{{r}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c68a910-d47a-5d42-8472-65a137268360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430736} {:answer/id #uuid "6f84c6cd-963a-5926-9438-431cae8fd173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430747} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c001e9-e046-5dfd-859e-0f7553c95834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430751} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c797d0-94a3-5402-8500-48167fa5bf9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430754} {:answer/id #uuid "f171ab4d-9dad-5bbc-b321-f960560419b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f171ab4d-9dad-5bbc-b321-f960560419b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c8566ad-8855-54ed-9229-fd53a23bc1a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430763}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2077302e-eca5-5aa1-8731-3c58ab648006" :question/math-academy-id "q-83520" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=x-y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/7b/7b181b47af7d29b68d86d26b5c2b948c17ce39c01a62cbb36e874bb733865718.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x-y\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\frac{1}{3}{[{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )({2}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d3fe42-617a-573e-8256-b7b54dddd6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707144} {:answer/id #uuid "402524b3-02af-57bf-81f9-85a8a17dfd64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707150} {:answer/id #uuid "b883197d-fdcc-58a5-a5d0-cc6450d2e1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707158} {:answer/id #uuid "deb817e9-d19e-567d-a1cc-40337e58f5a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707167} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3c3bd4-1cf9-5ee3-9417-3d2bc2bfe707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09d3fe42-617a-573e-8256-b7b54dddd6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707144} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf5a65d-9d36-5aa1-8d68-1065f45d41b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707159}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13a86bec-0b84-591c-82f2-f5db6466854c" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\ge0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d9/d919a1a51a86933bbe207c280000d3cb2a0af687445ad4f8a327db339715fed7.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 2 \\\\\n0\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 3 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{2}- 3 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[- \\frac{3 r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 12 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[12 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(12\\right) \\\\\n&=-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2adb339-63a0-58fe-8ce3-f85a07ceeef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707168} {:answer/id #uuid "e586f818-5484-5c16-bfc0-99a7f9f3e705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707169} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b09a80-58f3-50ca-9c02-d1f7360813ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707170} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e5b338-9cdd-5872-aa81-b350b453ff04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707172} {:answer/id #uuid "f83d2088-98f6-55f5-9c1b-b5f93916174d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f83d2088-98f6-55f5-9c1b-b5f93916174d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707177} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef32172-b166-5118-996c-8b182d490d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fd35840-1521-5928-a7bd-edd9c10ae1cb" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d0/d04f034896058356f7d978567dad992206e558d0cadad1f56ba3ab430d1b147e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x + 2 y\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{3}r^{2} \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^{3} \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)}{3}\\right]_0^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(9 \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 18 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(-9\\right)-\\left(18\\right) \\\\\n&=-27\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8978b6-0418-56c8-8445-205ca2337071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707145} {:answer/id #uuid "178e4a31-5f72-546a-87a5-ced4a7adcbe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707147} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb588bb-1765-5652-8e0f-5f9bc50d6c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707152} {:answer/id #uuid "c0dfe04d-fd26-5766-92cf-8dbc4c738666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707160} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e7e9ae-2e8c-5dbc-874f-6b1417136e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0dfe04d-fd26-5766-92cf-8dbc4c738666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707160} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc81b6b3-2187-5159-8b30-dcb13ce857ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707165}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eb8cbaa-d967-59ca-9b7d-59b6563688fb" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 4$ where $x\\ge0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/cf/cfcf5a05620442b1c529e896e6018540a976d1349c3c942f8b027ef6f757ae7f.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 2 \\\\\n- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\int_0^{2}- r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0}\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{0} \\\\\n&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06415642-22b6-5854-a375-f1d26c976885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{4} \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707143} {:answer/id #uuid "864cf11f-7152-5ce2-bbd0-0bbefe38d63a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707151} {:answer/id #uuid "a46f44a7-cc05-59d8-b95c-df37d1f1189c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707155} {:answer/id #uuid "a9418ee6-bdb7-5c61-a848-0a5384552abd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(\\frac{13}{4} \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707156} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fcffa7-c049-5fc6-af8c-a90a1a216d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6fcffa7-c049-5fc6-af8c-a90a1a216d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707171} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff82793-01f7-5ddf-9c61-ebb4fab99e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707157}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4fff9ef-f524-5cde-b212-eb1e587dd57e" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D \\frac{3}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 9$ where $x\\le0,\\ y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/d0/d04f034896058356f7d978567dad992206e558d0cadad1f56ba3ab430d1b147e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\nFor the radial primitive, set $u=r^2+1$, so $du=2r\\,dr$; its logarithm is defined since $r^2+1>0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\frac{3}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_0^{3}\\frac{3 r}{r^{2} + 1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(r^{2} + 1 \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 \\theta \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{9 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3 \\pi \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37999f4-ce84-5542-862a-d4679cff49b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707154} {:answer/id #uuid "c48a173f-87ff-5825-bf08-1bd747617c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707161} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f25bb2-93d0-5fa4-a348-b748284143db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707173} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c4e804-daf2-5750-a47b-5d4c5f25c642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707175} {:answer/id #uuid "f3eab12f-0e75-5ef5-aaaf-c9a2863c7857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a37999f4-ce84-5542-862a-d4679cff49b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707154} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd27ec8-40b2-5a92-967b-27bcc0768e5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707166}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf1f5562-b0fd-5986-9916-d0bd9456c231" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the portion of the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 1$ where $y\\le0$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/33/335dde7dc67f1b7f582c8e2f2d40e01271ce83d0f184fc86211c1171d244897e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 1 \\\\\n\\pi\\le\\theta&\\le 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_0^{1}- r^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\right]_0^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{\\theta}{4}\\right]_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0a7eb8-32f0-5041-bb4b-b64e54593884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707182} {:answer/id #uuid "3553c520-4652-599f-969a-4b9a7c34ad9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707184} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9aa42b-b8d7-5914-b75c-af2a59e809e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707186} {:answer/id #uuid "47b7a37b-9a44-59c1-8da8-0f2afbba1c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707187} {:answer/id #uuid "eac708bf-09aa-5a6b-af4f-4788bb3c4ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e0a7eb8-32f0-5041-bb4b-b64e54593884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707182} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3780e86-c2bf-59f4-8037-6aff0a1e2e94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707195}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b556547f-15db-5e0c-9db7-2706330ceabd" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - \\frac{y}{x}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $y\\ge0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=- 3 x$, shown below.\n\n![](images/eb/eb6ab6771940a63f6c826f7fef79305115b2f83f894e54c6d60f038982000833.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=-3x \\\\\nr^2&=-3r\\cos\\theta \\\\\nr&=- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/eb/eb6ab6771940a63f6c826f7fef79305115b2f83f894e54c6d60f038982000833.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le - 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe denominator vanishes only at boundary endpoints. Interpret angular endpoints as one-sided limits. Radial evaluation cancels the apparent singularity, leaving the continuous angular expression below, so the improper integral converges.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - \\frac{y}{x}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}- \\frac{r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right]_0^{- 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(- \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc1805f-ab7c-5de0-9967-175788955ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707183} {:answer/id #uuid "3dba34c0-bda2-5e04-88ab-4b3e338b824c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707185} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbcbeed-4dcb-520c-a974-c5a90d8214e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707189} {:answer/id #uuid "94c62ce8-c32a-529c-9d47-803982086de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707190} {:answer/id #uuid "ea350927-7f02-5598-a04f-29278ccee18c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1805f-ab7c-5de0-9967-175788955ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707183} :answer-field/id #uuid "0090c804-a233-5571-bc16-246016ab3ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707179}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c52223fb-4239-5990-b323-1745fb1866b1" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D \\frac{2 y}{x}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $y\\ge0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=- 2 x$, shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6c8c5af2bfe7fe3579510bceabdfdb9c40ed413eaba6a3aaaf9e90c30eb9b62.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=-2x \\\\\nr^2&=-2r\\cos\\theta \\\\\nr&=- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/f6/f6c8c5af2bfe7fe3579510bceabdfdb9c40ed413eaba6a3aaaf9e90c30eb9b62.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le - 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe denominator vanishes only at boundary endpoints. Interpret angular endpoints as one-sided limits. Radial evaluation cancels the apparent singularity, leaving the continuous angular expression below, so the improper integral converges.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\frac{2 y}{x}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\frac{2 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\right]_0^{- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274f7a57-c4a9-5eaf-9b99-58066f74c69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{972}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707181} {:answer/id #uuid "4b67d115-1e6f-54ae-a834-f9a3ccd6caa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{243}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707188} {:answer/id #uuid "993ea450-44ae-5f82-9672-db44727c5ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{243}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707191} {:answer/id #uuid "a874293e-28c1-5318-a148-7b199a2d4d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{972}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707192} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eca66c-fee2-5434-868c-e8d1d8904c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{320}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b67d115-1e6f-54ae-a834-f9a3ccd6caa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{243}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707188} :answer-field/id #uuid "105375d9-8a59-55a5-b1f4-0c9556d65470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707180}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec1914cb-c72a-57e4-9775-b5461c461baa" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D 3 x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the part of the disk where $x\\le0$ bounded by $x^2+y^2=3 y$, shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3ff147b4f3ab0dc7db522a812b6d8d7505506a28b5dc31d33a562184240c721e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^2+y^2&=3y \\\\\nr^2&=3r\\sin\\theta \\\\\nr&=3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\quad(r>0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe selected part of the circle gives the angular interval below; the origin is included at the radial lower bound.\n\n![](images/3f/3ff147b4f3ab0dc7db522a812b6d8d7505506a28b5dc31d33a562184240c721e.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta&\\le \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$. Since $r\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=r$.\nFor the angular primitive use $u=\\sin\\theta$, $du=\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,dA&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_0^{3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}3 r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left[\\frac{3 r^{4} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\left(\\frac{243 \\sin^{4}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{243 \\sin^{5}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{20}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(\\frac{243}{20}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{243}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455fd94f-078a-5487-b793-a7250aafb66e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707201} {:answer/id #uuid "cea2dab6-e4b8-523b-9826-4cc5f5a4ec33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707202} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ccf8dc-35ba-553c-9f8d-d30016e7d10f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707204} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a216e-f7f9-57fd-8819-7e15f0aefe31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707205} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb27b83-1377-5d60-abc1-1cf2de10faa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea2dab6-e4b8-523b-9826-4cc5f5a4ec33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707202} :answer-field/id #uuid "1481c56e-90a8-553f-ab6e-c3b998fd5ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707200}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da8bd0cc-88cb-501c-b46e-e45e964bc8a4" :question/problem "Evaluate \n\n$$\n\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA\n$$\n\n, where $D$ is the disk $x^2+y^2\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, with $r\\ge0$.\n\nThe Cartesian restrictions give the following polar region.\n\n![](images/4d/4d9d33bee82c23fc4e77f2f665931c7617f07fb5aa3ddbd0f8d09f3451fceecb.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le r&\\le 1 \\\\\n0\\le\\theta&\\le 2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $x^2+y^2=r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=r^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_0^{1}- r^{3}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[- \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\right]_0^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{\\theta}{4}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}]}
